{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FHNkHMwMYuwXJmMJ8oN23u","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Deep repository review — 2026-07-22\n2\t\n3\tThis is an as-found review of the live repository on branch\n4\t`dualplan-routing-fix` at commit `6a752b4`. It covers code quality,\n5\tarchitecture, requirements and prose, security, performance, tests,\n6\tdependencies, configuration, CI, Git practices, licensing, accessibility, and\n7\tinternationalization readiness.\n8\t\n9\tPer the request, historical/log material was excluded from the review corpus:\n10\t`docs/log.md`, `docs/archive/**`, `docs/iteration/**`, `docs/reviews/**`, and\n11\tthe owner-only `OWNER_SCRATCHPAD.md` were not audited. Generated OKF pages were\n12\tchecked through generator freshness rather than read one by one.\n13\t`PROJECT_STATE.html` was assessed through its generator and a fresh 36-image\n14\twidth/theme/tab render matrix. Existing repository-review reports were used\n15\tonly as a regression baseline; their conclusions were not copied forward\n16\twithout re-verification.\n17\t\n18\tGround truth at review time:\n19\t\n20\t- 728 tracked live-scope files; 58,445 Python lines across product scripts and\n21\t  tests, including 30,064 lines in the 34 shipped scripts.\n22\t- Full suite: **1,367 passed, 2 skipped in 275.66 s**.\n23\t- Smoke tier: **1,079 passed, 2 skipped in 221.43 s**.\n24\t- Ruff lint and format checks: clean. `compileall`: clean.\n25\t- Strict trace: **SN=25, SR=109, LLR=97, TC=100**, with zero orphan,\n26\t  integrity, schema, status, component, or interface findings.\n27\t- Latest coverage data: **92% overall**, but with materially weaker individual\n28\t  modules called out below.\n29\t- Duplicate detector: green with its allowlist; without that allowlist it\n30\t  reports **225 blocks across 101 allowlisted file pairs**.\n31\t- Secrets/privacy repository sweep: clean. No private-key material was found.\n32\t- Dashboard: 36/36 Playwright screenshots generated and representative light,\n33\t  dark, desktop, and 390 px images inspected. No clipping, contrast, or\n34\t  responsive-layout regression was visible.\n35\t- `npm audit --package-lock-only`: zero known vulnerabilities. Playwright\n36\t  1.61.1 is current. The compatible Ruff range already admits current\n37\t  0.15.22; pytest intentionally remains on the constrained 8.x line while a\n38\t  floating canary tests future majors.\n39\t\n40\t---\n41\t\n42\t## 1. Unfixed items and why\n43\t\n44\tThe as-found report was committed before remediation as `4db2ba3`. The\n45\tfindings below intentionally continue to describe the repository at `6a752b4`;\n46\tthis section records what changed afterward and what remains.\n47\t\n48\t### Fixed in this pass\n49\t\n50\t- **H-1 — fixed.** `agent_session.py` now rejects prompt-in-argv when the\n51\t  executable is, or resolves under the launch environment to, a Windows\n52\t  `.cmd`/`.bat` shim. The guard runs during template construction and again at\n53\t  the final process boundary. Stdin delivery remains allowed. Explicit-shim,\n54\t  environment-resolved-shim, and safe-stdin regressions were added, and the\n55\t  launcher/template/interface documentation no longer makes the false\n56\t  “never through a shell” claim.\n57\t- **M-1 — fixed in repository code and templates.** Both developer-workstation\n58\t  scripts and both one-shot project setup scripts now require Python 3.11+.\n59\t  Install modes refuse an existing older `.venv` instead of mutating it.\n60\t  POSIX behavior and the PowerShell contract have regression coverage. The\n61\t  existing local `.venv` is Python 3.9.6 and was deliberately not destroyed;\n62\t  it remains a workstation cleanup item, not a committed repository defect.\n63\t- **M-6 — fixed.** The live status/stack/session guidance now reports the\n64\t  measured ~3.3-minute smoke and ~4.2-minute full-suite bars, gives the exact\n65\t  documentation command, contains a genuinely forward-looking next action,\n66\t  documents all six WI states, and no longer describes completed WI-226 as the\n67\t  current owner of the complexity debt.\n68\t- **Dashboard defects were registered, not silently buried.** WI-272 records\n69\t  M-2 (status fidelity) and WI-273 records M-3 (semantic tabs), both as\n70\t  `deferred` rows with live report anchors.\n71\t- **Derived artifacts were regenerated.** The IF-041/IF-064 OKF pages and\n72\t  `PROJECT_STATE.html` now match the edited registries.\n73\t- **Budget-watched prose remained within its limits.** Before/after byte counts\n74\t  were: `AGENTS.template.md` 9,975 → 9,975; `PROCESS.md` 60,420 → 60,420;\n75\t  `PROCESS_OPTIONS.md` 161,007 → 160,980 (−27). No baseline restamp was needed.\n76\t\n77\t### Deferred, with reasons\n78\t\n79\t- **H-2 — core decomposition:** not safe as an opportunistic review cleanup.\n80\t  The long coordinators combine state transitions and external effects; a\n81\t  confident split requires dedicated WIs, behavioral characterization, typed\n82\t  state boundaries, and new size/complexity baselines. Moving the same long\n83\t  functions into more files would be cosmetic.\n84\t- **H-3 — license:** owner decision OI-4/WI-097. Choosing MIT, Apache-2.0,\n85\t  proprietary, or another grant changes legal rights and cannot be inferred by\n86\t  an engineering agent.\n87\t- **M-2 / WI-272 and M-3 / WI-273 — dashboard status fidelity and semantic\n88\t  tabs:** both are well-specified, but this repository requires a fresh,\n89\t  independent, family-heterogeneous render critique after dashboard changes.\n90\t  No independent reviewer was authorized/available in this session, so\n91\t  changing the render would knowingly violate the repository's own acceptance\n92\t  process.\n93\t- **M-4 — module coverage floors:** the weakest modules are identified, but\n94\t  choosing per-module thresholds without a focused coverage campaign would\n95\t  either bless weak current values or create arbitrary red gates.\n96\t- **M-5 — duplicate census redesign:** fingerprint/count baselines require a\n97\t  checker-format migration and careful treatment of the intentional standalone\n98\t  script duplication. A quick allowlist rewrite risks both false confidence and\n99\t  noisy false positives.\n100\t- **M-7 — branch integration and CI trigger policy:** opening/merging a PR and\n101\t  changing which remote branches consume CI are owner/repository-governance\n102\t  decisions. Rewriting the published 845-commit history would be destructive\n103\t  and is explicitly not recommended.\n104\t- **L-1 / L-2 — test and dashboard modularization:** should follow H-2's stable\n105\t  production seams; fragmenting tests or templates first would duplicate\n106\t  fixtures and move complexity without reducing it.\n107\t- **L-3 — historical Git metadata:** a history rewrite would be destructive and\n108\t  disproportionate. The current commit convention is already materially\n109\t  better.\n110\t- **L-4 — internationalization:** English-only behavior fits the currently\n111\t  stated product. Externalizing strings should start only if the owner adopts\n112\t  localization as a requirement/non-goal decision.\n113\t\n114\t**Closeout validation:** pre-final-test smoke **1,082 passed, 2 skipped in\n115\t200.24 s** and full suite **1,372 passed, 2 skipped in 247.47 s**; G3 harness\n116\t**PASS** across all 16 configured steps, including **91.52% coverage** with its\n117\tinstrumented run at **1,373 passed, 1 skipped in 429.42 s**. Two pure detector\n118\tregressions were then added; the final suite recorded **1,374 passed, 2 skipped\n119\tin 249.72 s**. Ruff format/lint, `compileall`,\n120\tstrict trace/trajectory, generated-artifact freshness, privacy, duplicate\n121\tdetection, skills sync, and documentation navigability all passed. The G3 run\n122\tused the existing unsupported local Python 3.9.6 environment noted above; the\n123\trepository's CI matrix remains the independent Python 3.11+/cross-platform\n124\tverification surface.\n125\t\n126\t---\n127\t\n128\t## 2. Executive summary\n129\t\n130\tThis repository is substantially better engineered than a typical template\n131\tkit. Its strongest feature is not any one script; it is the amount of\n132\tmechanized agreement between requirements, tests, generated views, and gates.\n133\tThe trace spine is clean, generated artifacts are freshness-checked, the CI\n134\tworkflow is SHA-pinned and least-privileged, tests are behavioral and\n135\tcross-platform, and the unattended coordinator contains unusually careful\n136\tfailure and recovery handling. The recent dashboard work also holds up\n137\tvisually across themes and widths.\n138\t\n139\tThe blunt assessment is that the repository's remaining risk is concentrated\n140\twhere the machines are weakest:\n141\t\n142\t1. A real Windows command-injection path remains in prompt delivery. The code\n143\t   says prompt substitution is safe because `shell=False`, but Windows can\n144\t   still route `.cmd`/`.bat` files through `cmd.exe` and reparse the substituted\n145\t   prompt.\n146\t2. The core is still too large and too cognitively expensive. Six production\n147\t   modules exceed 1,900 lines; 53 functions are deliberately grandfathered\n148\t   above the configured complexity limit. The ratchet prevents silent growth\n149\t   but does not make this code easy to reason about.\n150\t3. The project advertises a reusable copy-in kit without any license. That is\n151\t   a legal blocker, not cosmetic paperwork.\n152\t4. Several newer contracts are only partly implemented: the Python 3.11 floor\n153\t   is enforced by CI but not by the developer setup scripts; the dashboard\n154\t   loses `deferred`/`blocked` status information; and its visual tabs do not\n155\t   expose tab state to assistive technology.\n156\t5. Documentation currency slipped immediately after recent work. The status\n157\t   page still claims a 47-second smoke bar, teaches a noisier docs command, and\n158\t   puts completed work under “Next action.” The shipped README also describes\n159\t   only half of the six-state work-item lifecycle.\n160\t\n161\tThere are no Critical findings. The closest is H-1, but exploitation requires\n162\ta Windows batch-shim route plus attacker-influenced repository/prompt text in a\n163\tlocally launched unattended session. That is serious and should be fixed\n164\tfirst, but it is not a remotely exposed default service.\n165\t\n166\t---\n167\t\n168\t## 3. Prioritized findings\n169\t\n170\t### Critical\n171\t\n172\tNone found.\n173\t\n174\t### High\n175\t\n176\t#### H-1 · Windows batch-shim prompt delivery permits command injection\n177\t\n178\t- **Location:** `project-trajectory/scripts/agent_session.py:51-73`;\n179\t  `project-trajectory/agents.template.csv:5-9`\n180\t- **Relevant code:**\n181\t\n182\t  ```python\n183\t  for tok in split_cmd(template):\n184\t      if \"{prompt}\" in tok:\n185\t          saw_prompt = True\n186\t      argv.append(tok.replace(\"{model}\", model).replace(\"{prompt}\", prompt))\n187\t  return (argv, None) if saw_prompt else (argv, prompt)\n188\t  ```\n189\t\n190\t  ```csv\n191\t  GOOGLE-GEMINI-3-PRO,...,gemini -p {prompt} --output-format json,...\n192\t  AGENTS-EXAMPLE-000,...,your-cli -p {prompt},...\n193\t  ```\n194\t\n195\t- **Problem:** The comment “never through a shell” is false on Windows for\n196\t  batch files. `run_session()` deliberately resolves npm-style commands to\n197\t  `.cmd` shims. A prompt token containing `&`, `|`, `%NAME%`, quotes, or other\n198\t  `cmd.exe` syntax can therefore be reparsed as shell input even though Python\n199\t  was called with `shell=False`. Repository text is incorporated into prompts,\n200\t  so a malicious or merely unlucky prompt can cross this boundary.\n201\t- **Why it matters:** This is command execution inside the developer's\n202\t  checkout with the agent process's privileges. It undermines the otherwise\n203\t  careful model-slug validation and stdin transport work.\n204\t- **Suggested improvement:** Fail closed when `{prompt}` is used with an\n205\t  executable that explicitly is, or resolves on Windows to, `.cmd`/`.bat`.\n206\t  Require stdin prompt delivery or a native executable. Apply the check in\n207\t  preflight and again after environment-specific PATH resolution at launch.\n208\t  Add regressions for explicit and PATH-resolved batch shims and update the\n209\t  template guidance.\n210\t- **External confirmation:** Python's official subprocess documentation states\n211\t  that Windows batch files may be launched in a system shell regardless of\n212\t  the arguments passed to `subprocess`, with no escaping added by Python:\n213\t  [Python subprocess security considerations](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html#security-considerations).\n214\t\n215\t#### H-2 · Core module and function complexity remains beyond maintainable review scale\n216\t\n217\t- **Location:** `project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py` (4,511 lines),\n218\t  `agent_dispatch.py` (3,452), `agent_loop.py` (3,034), `trace.py` (2,206),\n219\t  `check_trajectory.py` (1,926), `bootstrap.py` (1,916);\n220\t  `tests/test_complexity_ratchet.py:37-111`\n221\t- **Relevant code/config:**\n222\t\n223\t  ```python\n224\t  (\"agent_dispatch.py\", \"dispatch_run\"): 40,\n225\t  (\"agent_loop.py\", \"session_bookkeeping\"): 31,\n226\t  (\"bootstrap.py\", \"main\"): 41,\n227\t  (\"plan_runner.py\", \"run_dual_plan_round\"): 30,\n228\t  (\"trace.py\", \"analyze\"): 53,\n229\t  (\"gen_trajectory.py\", \"sw_containment\"): 28,\n230\t  ```\n231\t\n232\t  Measured function sizes include `dispatch_run` at 398 lines,\n233\t  `agent_loop.main` at 393, `trace.analyze` at 383, `bootstrap.main` at 378,\n234\t  and `run_iteration` at 358.\n235\t- **Problem:** Fifty-three functions exceed the configured complexity ceiling\n236\t  of 10. The coordinator split improved file boundaries, but the central\n237\t  orchestration still passes large dictionary-shaped state through long\n238\t  procedures that mix decision logic, Git effects, process launching, artifact\n239\t  writes, and error disposition. `gen_trajectory.py` combines parsing,\n240\t  layout algorithms, HTML/CSS/JS templating, accessibility behavior, status\n241\t  generation, and CLI concerns in one file.\n242\t- **Why it matters:** These are the repository's highest-risk paths. A reviewer\n243\t  cannot hold all branches and invariants in working memory, and narrow edits\n244\t  routinely require complexity-baseline exceptions. The code can be green and\n245\t  still be difficult to change safely.\n246\t- **Suggested improvement:** Treat decomposition as architecture work, not a\n247\t  formatting exercise. Characterize state transitions first, introduce typed\n248\t  value objects for session/train/route state, isolate pure decisions from Git\n249\t  and subprocess effects, and split the dashboard into parsing, graph/layout,\n250\t  view-model, and rendering modules. Add a per-module size ratchet alongside\n251\t  the existing per-function complexity ratchet.\n252\t\n253\t#### H-3 · The reusable kit has no license\n254\t\n255\t- **Location:** repository root and `project-trajectory/`; no `LICENSE`,\n256\t  `COPYING`, or root legal notice exists. The decision is acknowledged in\n257\t  `docs/open-items.md` under OI-4 and deferred as WI-097.\n258\t- **Problem:** The README tells adopters to copy this kit into other\n259\t  repositories, but the repository grants no explicit right to copy, modify,\n260\t  or redistribute it.\n261\t- **Why it matters:** Default copyright is not an open-source license. External\n262\t  adoption is legally ambiguous, and every copied scaffold inherits that\n263\t  ambiguity. This is especially inconsistent in a kit that requires adopters\n264\t  to track asset licenses and attribution carefully.\n265\t- **Suggested improvement:** The owner must decide whether the repository is\n266\t  private, source-available, or open source. Then add the chosen license,\n267\t  copyright/notice text, README terms, and any required third-party notices.\n268\t  Do not guess MIT versus Apache-2.0 in an engineering cleanup.\n269\t\n270\t### Medium\n271\t\n272\t#### M-1 · The declared Python 3.11 floor is not enforced by developer setup\n273\t\n274\t- **Location:** `scripts/dev-setup.sh:73-84`,\n275\t  `scripts/dev-setup.ps1:37-60`,\n276\t  `project-trajectory/scripts/dev-setup.template.sh:149-151`,\n277\t  `project-trajectory/scripts/setup.sh:9-27`, and the PowerShell template\n278\t  equivalents.\n279\t- **Relevant code:**\n280\t\n281\t  ```sh\n282\t  if [ -x .venv/bin/python ]; then PY=.venv/bin/python\n283\t  elif real python3; then PY=python3\n284\t  elif real python; then PY=python\n285\t  fi\n286\t  report \"runtime (python3)\" \"$([ -n \"$PY\" ] && echo 1 || echo 0)\" \\\n287\t    \"install Python 3.11+\"\n288\t  ```\n289\t\n290\t- **Problem:** Any runnable Python is reported as `[ok]`. The live workspace\n291\t  demonstrates the defect: `.venv/bin/python` is 3.9.6, yet `dev-setup`\n292\t  accepts it and the full suite runs. `requirements-dev.txt` contains prose\n293\t  about the 3.11 floor but no enforceable interpreter constraint.\n294\t- **Why it matters:** Local verification can occur on an unsupported runtime,\n295\t  hiding 3.11-only failures until CI. The setup command explicitly promises to\n296\t  provision the supported environment and currently does not.\n297\t- **Suggested improvement:** Probe `sys.version_info >= (3, 11)` for every\n298\t  venv/ambient candidate in developer setup and project setup, report older\n299\t  interpreters as missing/unsupported, refuse install through an old\n300\t  interpreter, and regression-test both sides of the boundary in the shell\n301\t  and PowerShell templates.\n302\t\n303\t#### M-2 · The dashboard silently rewrites `deferred` and `blocked` work items as `queued`\n304\t\n305\t- **Location:** `project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py:489-504`,\n306\t  `:702-743`, `_wi_st()` near `:1214`; generated `PROJECT_STATE.html`\n307\t  currently serializes WI-271 as `\"status\": \"queued\"` although the registry\n308\t  says `deferred`.\n309\t- **Relevant code:**\n310\t\n311\t  ```python\n312\t  STATUS_FILL = {\n313\t      \"done\": \"#047857\",\n314\t      \"active\": \"#b45309\",\n315\t      \"queued\": \"#94a3b8\",\n316\t      \"retired\": \"#78716c\",\n317\t  }\n318\t  st = w[\"status\"] if w[\"status\"] in STATUS_FILL else \"queued\"\n319\t  details[w[\"id\"]] = {\"status\": st, ...}\n320\t  ```\n321\t\n322\t- **Problem:** The six-state source vocabulary is reduced to four states.\n323\t  This is not only a shared visual bucket: tooltips and the detail JSON report\n324\t  the wrong status. `deferred` means deliberately parked and `blocked` carries\n325\t  a named impediment; neither is equivalent to queued work.\n326\t- **Why it matters:** The dashboard is the repository's advertised unified\n327\t  state surface. Mislabeling parked work as ordinary queue work causes bad\n328\t  prioritization and contradicts the registry SSOT model.\n329\t- **Suggested improvement:** Preserve all six source statuses in details,\n330\t  labels, accessible names, and legend. If visual grouping is retained, name it\n331\t  explicitly and keep the actual status as a second field. Because this is a\n332\t  render-surface change, file it as its own dashboard WI and repeat the full\n333\t  width/theme/tab critique matrix.\n334\t\n335\t#### M-3 · Dashboard tabs have visual state but no tab semantics\n336\t\n337\t- **Location:** `project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py:2252-2280` and\n338\t  `:2418-2423`\n339\t- **Relevant code:**\n340\t\n341\t  ```html\n342\t  <nav class=\"tabs\">\n343\t    <button class=\"active\" data-tab=\"arch\">What (SR breakdown)</button>\n344\t    ...\n345\t  </nav>\n346\t  ```\n347\t\n348\t  ```javascript\n349\t  b.onclick = () => {\n350\t    for (const x of document.querySelectorAll('nav.tabs button'))\n351\t      x.classList.toggle('active', x===b);\n352\t    for (const p of document.querySelectorAll('.panel'))\n353\t      p.classList.toggle('active', p.id===b.dataset.tab);\n354\t  };\n355\t  ```\n356\t\n357\t- **Problem:** There is no `role=\"tablist\"`, `role=\"tab\"`,\n358\t  `aria-selected`, `aria-controls`, `role=\"tabpanel\"`, `aria-labelledby`,\n359\t  roving tabindex, or arrow-key behavior. Native buttons are reachable, but a\n360\t  screen reader is not told which view is selected or how buttons and panels\n361\t  relate.\n362\t- **Why it matters:** The active view is core state, not decoration. Visual-only\n363\t  selection excludes assistive-technology users and weakens the otherwise good\n364\t  keyboard/accessibility work in the graphs.\n365\t- **Suggested improvement:** Implement the ARIA tabs pattern, synchronize\n366\t  selected/hidden/focus state, and add keyboard regressions for arrows,\n367\t  Home/End, Enter/Space, and panel visibility. File and validate it as its own\n368\t  render WI.\n369\t\n370\t#### M-4 · The global coverage floor hides weak high-risk modules\n371\t\n372\t- **Location:** `docs/stack.ini:45-51` (`threshold = 85`); latest coverage\n373\t  report.\n374\t- **Evidence:** Overall coverage is 92%, but\n375\t  `subagent_gate.py` is 40%, `plan_coverage_step.py` 65%,\n376\t  `agent_session.py` 74%, `plan_runner.py` 79%, and `run_menu.py` 79%.\n377\t- **Problem:** One aggregate threshold permits heavily tested generators and\n378\t  trace code to subsidize thin coverage in process-launch, policy-gate, and\n379\t  planning adapters. The headline percentage overstates confidence in those\n380\t  modules.\n381\t- **Why it matters:** `agent_session.py` contains the process and security\n382\t  boundary implicated by H-1. `subagent_gate.py` is itself a safety control.\n383\t  Branches missed there are not interchangeable with lines covered elsewhere.\n384\t- **Suggested improvement:** Add per-module minimums for security/process\n385\t  boundaries, initially at honest current baselines, then raise them with\n386\t  focused behavioral tests. Keep the global floor as a backstop rather than the\n387\t  only measure.\n388\t\n389\t#### M-5 · Duplicate-code enforcement is too coarse to detect growth inside 101 trusted pairs\n390\t\n391\t- **Location:** `docs/dupes-allow`; `project-trajectory/scripts/check_dupes.py`\n392\t- **Relevant policy text:**\n393\t\n394\t  ```text\n395\t  Paths are allowed by line-number-free \"a.py == b.py\" file pairs.\n396\t  New copy-paste between two files ALREADY listed here is not caught.\n397\t  ```\n398\t\n399\t- **Problem:** The allowlist currently hides 225 detected blocks across 101\n400\t  file pairs. Much of it is a deliberate “standalone script” policy, but the\n401\t  pair-level exemption means any future duplicate block between an already\n402\t  listed pair passes automatically. It is a widening trust hole, not a fixed\n403\t  census.\n404\t- **Why it matters:** The repository's F5 duplication exception is large enough\n405\t  that accidental clones can disappear inside it. It also makes the green\n406\t  duplicate check easy to misread as “no duplication.”\n407\t- **Suggested improvement:** Key exemptions by normalized token fingerprint\n408\t  plus file pair, or keep a count/hash baseline per pair. Continue permitting\n409\t  small standalone loaders where justified, but make new blocks require a\n410\t  deliberate baseline change.\n411\t\n412\t#### M-6 · Live operational documentation contradicts current behavior\n413\t\n414\t- **Location:** `docs/status.md:37-43`; `docs/status.md` “Next action”;\n415\t  `docs/stack.ini:18-23`; `project-trajectory/README.md:36`;\n416\t  `tests/test_complexity_ratchet.py:18,56,104`.\n417\t- **Evidence:**\n418\t\n419\t  ```markdown\n420\t  smoke ... (~47 s)\n421\t  check_docs.py --root . --stale\n422\t  ```\n423\t\n424\t  Current smoke is 221.43 s. The direct docs command also omits the\n425\t  `--ignore doc